\title{Machine speed and the pipeline: default switch set, warm passes, parallel loading}
\taxon{Session}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{driver}
\tag{loader}
\tag{performance}
\meta{harness}{Claude Code}
\meta{model-id}{claude-fable-5-2}
\import{base-macros}

\p{The fifth phase ran through most of 2026-11-24: the package owners of [the machine](ses-Z72N) made it faster switch by switch, the pipeline's owners made the driver, the loader and the launcher fit the new target, and an integrator chose a default switch set and gated it. Branch \code{dev}, from commit \commit{b37d5a4} on; the commit messages of the day name each step. The team grew during this phase, and two host-wide tools were written after the development host ran out of memory under it: \code{\verb~|bench/slot.sh~} limits heavy jobs, and \code{\verb~|bench/quiet.py~} serialises timing runs on reserved performance cores. A wall-clock number counts as evidence only when it was taken through the second.}

\p{\strong{The target changed during the phase.} The maintainer made warm wall clock with parallel workers the goal, see [standing policies](standing-policies). The launcher and the driver got \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~}, with which one process checks the same export again, either reloading it or re-checking the loaded store, and \code{\verb~|bench/warm.sh~} reports the passes. \src{docs/BENCHMARKS.md} carries the warm tables.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Machine.} The value store's first stage; a memo for closed terms and an economy of arguments; beta bodies evaluated without a tabling record (\code{bodies}); derived reduction rules (\code{directrules}); case trees with inlined matchers (\code{casetrees}, \code{lazycases}); code objects for reduction (\code{code}) with rent-to-own thresholds per role; a cache at the front of inference; a bounded structural identity (\code{same}); a one-sided unfolding run as one reduction (\code{lazyeval}); a bound on the ticks of one unit, with which the two arena tests on which the official kernel does not terminate are declined in seconds. Each switch has its gate and its measurement in \src{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md}.}
\li{\strong{Universe levels.} Both engines compare levels as the official kernel does by default; the complete relation is the opt-in \code{\verb~|levels=complete~}. Admission rebuilds the levels of inductive blocks the official way, checked against 2,200 and 1,468 official verdicts.}
\li{\strong{Driver.} A session cap per engine, scheduler slices, a fix of the environment thread, a parking rule with which a pipelined load decides what a batch load decides, engines kept across passes, and an account of where each worker's wall clock goes. The check phase scales 6.9 to 7.4 times with 8 workers; see \src{docs/research/profiles/P7.md}.}
\li{\strong{Loader.} Proof runs are packed by scanner threads in parallel, and duplicate level-parameter records are declined. With two scanner threads the load alone is 1.8 times shorter on Std and cedar and 1.6 times on Mathlib (5.8 to 6.2 s against 9.2 to 9.6 s, on the JVM). A warm pass with 8 workers on 8 cores does not get shorter by it; see \src{rlvm-load/README.md}.}
\li{\strong{Platform.} \src{docs/PLATFORM.md}: for a fresh process per export, which is what the arena runs, the native image with the machine as engine needs 0.68, 0.46 and 0.37 times the JVM launcher's CPU time with 1, 4 and 8 workers. The warm tables then showed the JVM launcher ahead once warm on Mathlib, which reopened the platform question under the new target.}
}

\p{\strong{The default switch set.} The integrator fixed a difference of one tick between the two control conventions in the code objects, ran the hostile exports under 32 switch sets, the mutants, the full gate and the differential engine over 760,839 units without a difference from the reference engine, and published the machine with the switch set \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,bodies,pchk,match,joint,code,casetrees,lazycases,directrules,lazyeval,same~}, a session cap of 1 MiB and a host depth bound of 128. With it the machine was made the default engine at 19:41 UTC.}

\p{\strong{Numbers of that distribution}, from the benchmark tables as they stood then, \srcat{e021aef}{docs/BENCHMARKS.md}. Warm wall clock, 8 workers, pipelined loading, on the same cores of the aarch64 development host in the same windows: Mathlib 27.4 s as a native image and 21.2 s on the JVM launcher, against 12.3 s for gruszka and 18.5 s for nano-lean; Init 0.81 s against 0.66 s and 1.04 s; Std 1.51 s against 1.06 s and 1.74 s; cedar 2.08 s against 1.65 s and 2.69 s; reachability 0.61 s against 2.36 s and 0.75 s. Against the first warm table of the same day, taken with the first three switches, Mathlib went from 35.3 s to 27.4 s as a native image. In the arena-shaped container the 230 published tests took 63.9 s of CPU time through the arena's run line, where the reference pipeline had taken 154 s.}

\p{\strong{Taken back.} At 20:35 UTC the machine was set back to a non-default priority. An agent writing new hostile exports for a prototype had found accepts of the machine that the official checker rejects: a bound variable was typed by the value in its slot where the official kernel uses the binder's declared type. Every gate of the integration had compared the machine with the reference engine on corpora and mutants, where the two types coincide, or with hostile exports that had no row of this shape. The integrator's own conclusion: no difference on 760,839 units was true and said nothing about it, and making an engine the default needs a review with new hostile exports first. The fix and what followed are in [the exactness phase](ses-874D).}

\p{\strong{Decisions of this phase.} [The metric is warm wall clock](dec-7QMU); [universe levels as the official kernel compares them](dec-P8XP); [no Oracle GraalVM in anything default](dec-6E82); [the default switch set](dec-LZFX) and [the session cap of 1 MiB](dec-KXE1); [the machine as default engine, made and withdrawn](dec-T79V); [parallel packing of proof runs](dec-OC1F) and [pipelined loading as the default for parallel runs](dec-4TQJ); [the JVM launcher as the warm platform and the native image as the cold one](dec-WX9Z). Design notes: [switches](design-switches), [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions), [the launchers](design-launchers), [the measurement method](design-measurement-method).}

\p{\strong{Policy recorded.} The maintainer's decisions on universe levels, Oracle GraalVM, commits and the size of the team, and the change of the goal to warm wall clock, in [standing policies](standing-policies).}

\p{\strong{Running beside this phase.} [Profiles and prototypes](ses-JRSD).}
